      </sec>
    </body>
    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-18721.s24">
        <fig id="article-18721.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Punctal Stenosis.&#x000a0;The image depicts conditions&#x000a0;resulting in lacrimal drainage abnormalities.&#x000a0;Panel A shows punctal stenosis caused by a chemical burn with conjunctival and lid margin mucocutaneous junction scarring. Panel B displays complete obliteration of the puncta secondary to cicatrization caused by Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Panel C illustrates punctal occlusion in the presence of mucous membrane pemphigoid disease. Contributed by Professor Bhupendra C. K. Patel MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="4.__Burn__SJ__Pemphigoid" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18721.s25">
        <fig id="article-18721.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Dua Classification of Ocular Surface Burns. This chart provides a standardized method for assessing the severity of chemical and thermal ocular burns. The Dua system correlates the physical extent of tissue damage, specifically limbal and conjunctival scarring, with the expected clinical outcome for the patient. Contributed by Dr Lanxing Fu</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Dua__Classification" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18721.s26">
        <fig id="article-18721.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Digital Slit Lamp Examination of Ocular Thermal Burn. This image shows a patient with a thermal burn presenting with lower lid ectropion, diffuse conjunctival congestion, and hyperemia. Clinical findings also include chemosis, mucopurulent discharge in the lower tarsal conjunctiva, and an inferior 1/3 corneal anterior stromal infiltrate suggestive of exposure keratopathy. Contributor- Dr. Bharat Gurnani, MBBS, DNB, FCRS, FICO, MRCS Ed, MNAMS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Burns__ectropion__EXP__keratopathy__699454_20191227_084713_DC4Image_L_014" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18721.s27">
        <fig id="article-18721.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Digital Slit Lamp Image of Ocular Chemical Burn. This image shows a patient with a lime-induced chemical burn presenting with matted lashes, diffuse conjunctival congestion, and blanching. Clinical findings also include stromal edema and inferior limbal stem cell deficiency, with partially hazy iris and pupil details suggestive of a grade III chemical injury. Contributor- Dr. Bharat Gurnani, MBBS, DNB, FCRS, FICO, MRCS Ed, MNAMS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Ocular__chemical__burn" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18721.s28">
        <fig id="article-18721.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Microscopic Pathogenesis of Ocular Burns. Chemical or thermal injury disrupts the corneal epithelium and stroma, triggering inflammation, ischemia, and stem cell loss at the limbus. Ongoing cytokine release and matrix metalloproteinase (MMP) activation drive stromal degradation, fibrosis, and corneal neovascularization. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Fig__1" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18721.s29">
        <fig id="article-18721.image.f6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Pathophysiology of Ocular Burns. Chemical or thermal exposure causes immediate epithelial injury, followed by stromal penetration, limbal ischemia, and loss of limbal stem cells. Subsequent inflammatory cytokine release and matrix metalloproteinase (MMP) activation lead to corneal melting, scarring, and long-term visual sequelae. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Fig__2" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18721.s30">
        <fig id="article-18721.image.f7" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Clinical Evaluation Pathway in Ocular Burns. The diagram outlines a stepwise approach to ocular burn evaluation, beginning with exposure history and immediate irrigation. Assessment includes visual acuity, corneal and conjunctival integrity, limbal ischemia, anterior chamber inflammation, intraocular pressure (IOP), and posterior segment evaluation to guide severity grading and management. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Fig__3" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18721.s31">
        <fig id="article-18721.image.f8" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Recommended Protocol for the Evaluation of Ocular Burns. The diagram presents a sequential protocol for evaluating ocular burns following irrigation and pH neutralization (7.0&#x02013;7.4). Key stages include visual acuity testing using Snellen charts, logarithm of the minimum angle of resolution (logMAR), light perception, and projection of rays; comprehensive slit lamp biomicroscopy to identify limbal ischemia and epithelial defects; intraocular pressure measurement to detect hypotony or elevation; posterior segment evaluation with B-scan ultrasonography when the view is obscured; optional use of anterior segment optical coherence tomography or computed tomography for detection of foreign bodies or severity grading; and final severity assignment according to the Roper&#x02013;Hall and Dua classification systems, in accordance with recommendations from the American Academy of Ophthalmology and Royal College of Ophthalmologists. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Fig__4" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
